/-
Fmt/M1FilesT.lean: test data for a verifier, namely proof files of the first toy (a product test) at
the programs' tiny parameters, as bytes: one honest file and 45 bad ones.

Definitions, and tests (#guard): the files' sizes; no two files have the same bytes. Made by a
program from the files and from the table that came with them. The data are made-up toy data: no
model and no weights. No Mathlib.

This library's own, not the programs' (the programs: the C++ implementation that comes with this
Lean code). The files were made outside Lean by a reference of this library's own in Python (an
honest prover, cheating
provers, a verifier), written from the programs' description of the toy and from the format page's
own reference program (format_rules.py; first in an edition of the format page that is not the one
that ships; the files here were made with that program in a second edition, also not the one that
ships; not held against that page), at the sizes K = 4; stages (main code, mask code, k, h,
positions)
((7, 16, 3), (7, 16, 3), 2, 0, 3), ((8, 8, 3), (8, 32, 3), 1, 1, 3),
((9, 8, 5), (9, 128, 5), 0, 0, 5); 6 bits of work.
No file here comes from the programs, and where their description is silent the reference has made
choices of its own.
The first toy is a test protocol for the claim that three rows a, b, c of one oracle message have
a_i b_i = c_i for every i (the heads of Fmt/M1Types.lean and Fmt/M1Psc.lean).

Main names:
  `Dn`: which of the five parts of the decision refuses:
    d1 form; d2 work; d3 openings; d4 the last block's linear check; d5 its checks at the
    positions.
  `honest`; the bad files, each under its name, with what was done to it in the words of the table.
  `files`: all of them but the re-cut ones; `recut`: the re-cut ones. An entry has name, bytes, and
    nothing (`none`) for "accepted" or the part that refuses first in the reference verifier.

How the decision is divided: the head of Fmt/M1Types.lean.

The kinds of bad files.
  alt: bytes of the honest file altered.
  cheat: a cheating prover: one word of one message is made one larger before it enters its query,
    all that follows is made by the honest rules.
  false: a false statement proved by the honest rules.
  word: no code word: one word of a column is altered before the tree is built, at a position that
    is then drawn.
  recut: a nonce that fails the work test of one round, all that follows by the honest rules.

Limits: this file does not say that a reader or a decision accepts or refuses the files. For the
reader, see the tests of Fmt/M1PlanKnownT.lean.

The bytes of the files that a cheating prover has made (cheat, false, word, recut), and the type
`Dn`, are in Fmt/MustBeRefused/M1FilesT.lean, each with its expected refusal beside it. The
lists here name them. The folder's name, MustBeRefused, says what its files hold.

Terms: lean/GLOSSARY.md.
-/
import Fmt.MustBeRefused.M1FilesT

namespace Fmt
namespace M1
namespace FilesT

/-- the honest file -/
def honest : ByteArray := unhex ""

/-- byte 0 of the random bytes flipped -/
def alt_01 : ByteArray := unhex ""

/-- byte 0 of the root of tree 0 flipped -/
def alt_02 : ByteArray := unhex ""

/-- byte 0 of the first nonce flipped -/
def alt_03 : ByteArray := unhex ""

/-- byte 0 of a column of tree 0 flipped -/
def alt_04 : ByteArray := unhex ""

/-- byte 0 of a salt of tree 1 flipped -/
def alt_05 : ByteArray := unhex ""

/-- byte 0 of a given digest of tree 2 flipped -/
def alt_06 : ByteArray := unhex ""

/-- byte 0 of a column of tree 2 flipped -/
def alt_07 : ByteArray := unhex ""

/-- the file one byte short -/
def alt_08 : ByteArray := unhex ""

/-- the file one byte long (one byte more than `honest`) -/
def alt_09 : ByteArray := unhex ""

/-- the first word of y is p -/
def alt_10 : ByteArray := unhex ""

/-- the first word of the first opened column of tree 0 is p (no spelling of an element) -/
def alt_11a : ByteArray := unhex ""

/-- the first word of the first opened column of tree 1 is p (no spelling of an element) -/
def alt_11b : ByteArray := unhex ""

/-- the first word of the first opened column of tree 2 is p (no spelling of an element) -/
def alt_11c : ByteArray := unhex ""

/-- All files but the re-cut ones. -/
def files : List (String × ByteArray × Option Dn) :=
  [("honest", honest, none),
   ("alt-01", alt_01, some .d2),
   ("alt-02", alt_02, some .d2),
   ("alt-03", alt_03, some .d2),
   ("alt-04", alt_04, some .d3),
   ("alt-05", alt_05, some .d3),
   ("alt-06", alt_06, some .d3),
   ("alt-07", alt_07, some .d3),
   ("alt-08", alt_08, some .d1),
   ("alt-09", alt_09, some .d1),
   ("alt-10", alt_10, some .d1),
   ("cheat-01", cheat_01, some .d4),
   ("cheat-02", cheat_02, some .d4),
   ("cheat-03", cheat_03, some .d4),
   ("cheat-04", cheat_04, some .d4),
   ("cheat-05", cheat_05, some .d4),
   ("cheat-06", cheat_06, some .d4),
   ("cheat-07", cheat_07, some .d4),
   ("cheat-08", cheat_08, some .d4),
   ("cheat-09", cheat_09, some .d4),
   ("cheat-10", cheat_10, some .d4),
   ("cheat-11", cheat_11, some .d4),
   ("cheat-12", cheat_12, some .d4),
   ("cheat-13", cheat_13, some .d4),
   ("cheat-14", cheat_14, some .d4),
   ("cheat-15", cheat_15, some .d4),
   ("cheat-16", cheat_16, some .d4),
   ("cheat-17", cheat_17, some .d4),
   ("cheat-18", cheat_18, some .d5),
   ("cheat-19", cheat_19, some .d5),
   ("false-01", false_01, some .d4),
   ("word-0-03", word_0_03, some .d4),
   ("word-0-06", word_0_06, some .d4),
   ("word-1-00", word_1_00, some .d4),
   ("word-1-06", word_1_06, some .d4),
   ("word-1-12", word_1_12, some .d4),
   ("word-2-00", word_2_00, some .d5),
   ("word-2-03", word_2_03, some .d5),
   ("word-2-06", word_2_06, some .d5),
   ("word-2-09", word_2_09, some .d5),
   ("alt-11a", alt_11a, some .d1),
   ("alt-11b", alt_11b, some .d1),
   ("alt-11c", alt_11c, some .d1)]

/-- The re-cut files: a nonce that fails the work test of one round, and all that follows by the
honest rules at what that nonce's answer draws: a reader that does not test the work cuts them; the
work test alone can refuse: this says how they are made, and this file tests neither. In
Run/M1RunT.lean a test has it that the lenient reader cuts them and the decision accepts them. -/
def recut : List (String × ByteArray × Option Dn) :=
  [("recut-16", recut_16, some .d2),
   ("recut-20", recut_20, some .d2),
   ("recut-22", recut_22, some .d2)]

#guard (files ++ recut).map (fun x => x.2.1.size) = [7936, 7936, 7936, 7936, 7936, 7936, 7936, 7936, 7935, 7937, 7936, 7808, 7808, 7648, 7808, 7936, 7744, 7808, 7712, 8000, 7936, 7840, 7648, 7608, 7776, 7872, 7840, 7904, 7840, 7904, 7904, 7648, 7872, 8000, 7680, 7776, 7840, 7680, 7776, 7680, 7936, 7936, 7936, 7616, 7840, 7776]
-- no two files are the same bytes
#guard (files ++ recut).all fun x => ((files ++ recut).filter fun y => y.2.1 = x.2.1).length = 1

end FilesT
end M1
end Fmt
